vice captured, systemctl reboot acknowledged, boot_id observed to change from 739c9792... to 20936883... (also cross-checked by the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot), and post-restart state shows inventory-api enabled/active, serving correct JSON, and oom_score_adj=-1000 on the new process.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-42e9fccb0feb4f26\",\"cmd-695a3effa4c349d9\",\"cmd-5bcaab1b37564fcc\",\"cmd-cd535c678c5e42f6\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.93,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map to a clear task purpose: app.py and the systemd unit (required deliverable), the memory-hog script (bounded, necessary to demonstrate the OOM behavior, and its files were explicitly removed in cmd-42e9fccb0feb4f26 after the process itself was reaped by the kernel), and the reboot for the mandated restart protocol. No abandoned files, failed services, or weakened security controls are present in the after_executor lifecycle snapshot (failed-services empty, package-health OK, SELinux unaffected). Minor residue: /tmp/reboot.log left over from the reboot trigger redirect, a low-risk, expected artifact of the chosen reboot method rather than an unnecessary mutation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6717e487ce2b4f95\",\"cmd-42e9fccb0feb4f26\",\"cmd-695a3effa4c349d9\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor deployed inventory-api as a systemd-managed service on node1 with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000/OOMPolicy=continue, then directly demonstrated the required behavior end to end: launched a real unbounded memory hog that drove the system into an actual kernel OOM event (confirmed via dmesg), showed the kernel killed the hog (pid 1715, oom_score_adj=0) while inventory-api (oom_score_adj=-1000) kept running with zero restarts and continued serv